  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  PART A -- THE DSPM-DE PORE SOLVED TWICE, BY TWO PROGRAMS: Choupo's
  finite-difference pore against NanoFiltran (Geraldes & Alves, "Computer
  program for simulation of mass transport in nanofiltration membranes",
  J. Membr. Sci. 321 (2008) 172-182), Vitor Geraldes's own program, on the
  SAME model and the SAME inputs.

  WHAT KIND OF CHECK THIS IS.  CODE-TO-CODE VERIFICATION: one model, two
  independent implementations (NanoFiltran: Fortran 77, central
  differences, under-relaxed Newton on a dense matrix; Choupo: C++,
  exponentially fitted fluxes, Newton on a block-tridiagonal matrix).  It
  says the equations are solved right.  It is NOT a validation: no number
  here is a measurement, and the membrane is NanoFiltran's worked example,
  which describes no real membrane.

  THE MODEL BOTH SOLVE.  The extended Nernst-Planck equations across the
  pore with electroneutrality at every node; steric (1 - lambda)^2, Born
  dielectric and Donnan partitions at both faces; the Dechadilok & Deen
  (2006) hindrance pair; the multi-ionic interface balance of Geraldes &
  Afonso (2007) with the suction correction of Geraldes & Afonso (2006);
  the wall closed with the pore's own permeate.  Every one of those is
  DECLARED below (`transportParameters`), so this case does not depend on
  Choupo's defaults; since 2026-10-24 (DEV.md 4c C141) the hindrance pair,
  the solved pore and that wall closure ARE the defaults, while the
  activities and the bulk dielectric constant stay NanoFiltran's declared
  choices (docs/design/a-stiff-pore-solved-at-once.md, sections 9 and 10,
  list every difference and how each was handled).
  "No polarisation" is NanoFiltran's own: a film of k = 10 m/s.

  THE SIX OPERATIONS (NanoFiltran's matrix, bin/curate/nanofiltran/):
    example_ideal    NanoFiltran's shipped example (Na+/Mg2+/Cl- 10/20/50
                     mol/m3, X -30 mol/m3, r_p 0.4 nm, 2.5 um, eps_p 60),
                     ideal activities, J_v 1e-6 .. 5e-5 m/s
    example_noBorn   the same with eps_p = 80 (no dielectric exclusion)
    example_davies   example_noBorn with Davies activities at both faces
                     (bulk/pore ratio; see the Davies finding below)
    NaCl_ideal       a 1:1 salt, 10 mol/m3
    Na2SO4_ideal     a 2:1 salt whose DIVALENT CO-ION meets the negative pore
    mixed_polarised  NaCl + Na2SO4 10/10 mol/m3 with concentration
                     polarisation: Sh = 0.23 Re^0.567 Sc^0.33 per ion,
                     Re 2000, L 2.4 cm (Choupo's StirredCell with radius = L,
                     density 997 kg/m3 and NanoFiltran's own Vogel
                     viscosity at 298.15 K, so k_c,i is the same number)

  WHAT IS PINNED.  `expected` carries NanoFiltran's permeate concentrations
  as ANCHOR rows -- its Richardson extrapolation of ND 102 and 204, the
  program's limit -- with tolerances sized from the MEASURED discretisation
  error of both programs: this case runs 801 nodes, whose error against the
  converged pore is the larger term.  bin/curate/check_nanofiltran_reference.py
  holds the tighter claim: Choupo extrapolated from 401/801/1601 nodes agrees
  with NanoFiltran extrapolated to ~1e-7 relative on every row.

  THREE THINGS IN NANOFILTRAN ARE CONSTANTS, NOT MODEL, and are matched
  through its INPUTS (its source is run as received): its diffusivity is
  corrected to T by its Vogel viscosity (1.00014 at 298.15 K); its Born
  constant is the rounded 6.95e-5/8.314; its hindrance polynomial is written
  in single-precision literals, which at lambda = 0.875 (Mg2+) moves K_d by
  1.55e-4 -- uncorrected, that alone parts the two programs by 5.5e-5 on
  MgCl2 and 6.8e-4 on the trace Na+ of the example.

  THE DAVIES FINDING.  NanoFiltran evaluates Davies as exp(-A z^2 (...))
  with A = 1.825e6 (eps T)^-1.5 -- the log10 constant (0.495 at 80, 298 K)
  -- so its ln gamma is ln 10 = 2.303 times too small.  Choupo's
  `activity bulkOverPoreDavies` is Davies on log10; its rows are anchored
  on NanoFiltran built with the two lines that compute A corrected
  (DAVIES_PATCH in regenerate.py: ln 10, and the constant to five figures,
  1.82483e6).  As received, the program puts 7.7 % more Na+ in this op's
  permeate; that number is reported (`regenerate.py --report-davies`), not
  pinned.  The op runs with Born OFF because, with it on, matching
  NanoFiltran's Born constant through its pore dielectric input would also
  move its pore-side Davies A by 2e-4 -- measured to part the trace Na+ by
  5.5e-4 -- and the two effects could not be told apart.
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  PART B -- THE DSPM-DE PORE AGAINST EXPERIMENT: Bowen & Mohammad's
  CA30 membrane, sodium chloride with a trivalent dye, as the NanoFiltran
  paper compares it.
    W.R. Bowen, A.W. Mohammad, "Diafiltration by nanofiltration: prediction
      and optimization", AIChE J. 44 (1998) 1799 -- the MEASUREMENTS;
    V. Geraldes, A.M. Brites Alves, "Computer program for simulation of mass
      transport in nanofiltration membranes", J. Membr. Sci. 321 (2008)
      172-182, doi:10.1016/j.memsci.2008.04.054 -- section 4.1 and Fig. 3,
      where NanoFiltran is set against them.

  WHAT KIND OF CHECK THIS IS.  A COMPARISON WITH EXPERIMENT -- the first in
  this corpus for the solved DSPM-DE pore -- and it must be read for what
  it is: SEVERAL INPUTS ARE FITS TO THESE VERY DATA, by Bowen & Mohammad.
  The pore radius and the equivalent thickness (0.63 nm, 6.2 um) were
  determined from them; the membrane charge is their fitted function of the
  feed (Eq. 43 below); the permeate flux their fitted function of the feed
  at each dye level (Eqs. 44-45).  So agreement here says the pore model,
  carried by inputs fitted on these data, reproduces them -- it does NOT
  say the model PREDICTS a membrane it was not fitted on.  Nothing was
  fitted by Choupo.

  THE CASE (the paper's section 4.1): Na+, Cl- and a trivalent dye (Stokes
  radius 0.63 nm = the pore radius, so lambda = 1 and the pore EXCLUDES it:
  it acts through the film and the electroneutrality there), 298 K,
  500 kPa, a stirred cell (stirrer radius 0.024 m, Re 26 900) with
      Sh = 0.23 Re^0.567 Sc^0.33              (the paper's Eq. 46)
  -- exactly Choupo's `StirredCell`, with NanoFiltran's own Vogel viscosity
  at 298 K and density 997 kg/m3, omega chosen so that Re = 26 900 --
      c_X = -9.03 - 3.29 (3 c_Dye + c_Cl)      [mol/m3]   (Eq. 43)
      J_v = 9.91e-6 (1 - 0.0011 c_Cl)                  [m/s] at 4 mol/m3 of dye  (Eq. 44)
      J_v = 4.13e-6 (1 + 0.00981 c_Cl - 7.46e-5 c_Cl^2) [m/s] at 37 mol/m3      (Eq. 45)
  ideal activities, eps_p = eps_b (no dielectric exclusion), the
  multi-ionic film of Geraldes & Afonso (2007) with its suction correction
  (NanoFiltran's), the Dechadilok-Deen hindrance.  Ions: Na+ 0.184 nm /
  1.33e-9 m2/s, Cl- 0.121 nm / 2.03e-9, the dye 0.63 nm / 0.36e-9 -- the
  paper's values.  Every op below writes its own c_X and J_v with the
  arithmetic beside it; bin/curate/check_nanofiltran_paper.py recomputes
  them from the equations.

  THE TEN OPERATIONS are the ten measurements of Fig. 3 (five chloride
  levels at each dye level).  The feed chloride of each is READ OFF THE
  FIGURE: constant/experimental/geraldes_alves2008_fig3.csv, digitised by
  bin/curate/digitise_geraldes_alves2008.py (calibration checked against
  the tick labels, reading error +-1.5 px = 0.32 mol/m3 and 0.011 in R_obs;
  the paper and its figure are the publisher's and are not in the tree).
  An op's c_Cl is the mean of its two markers' readings (the Na+ and Cl-
  symbols of one measurement sit at one feed).

  WHAT IS PINNED.  `expected`'s anchor rows are the MEASURED permeates,
  c_p = c_b (1 - R_obs) of each symbol, with bands sized to the measured
  disagreement; the self-recorded rows pin that Choupo's answer has not
  moved.  bin/curate/check_nanofiltran_paper.py also holds Choupo to
  NanoFiltran on the same inputs (code to code, 1e-6), and NanoFiltran with
  the paper's own settings to the paper's drawn line (the inputs read right).

