  PART C -- THE STIFF CASE OF THE NANOFILTRAN PAPER, AND ITS TABLE 2.
  V. Geraldes, A.M. Brites Alves, "Computer program for simulation of mass
  transport in nanofiltration membranes", J. Membr. Sci. 321 (2008)
  172-182, doi:10.1016/j.memsci.2008.04.054, section 4.2: Na+/Mg2+/Cl-
  10/20/50 mol/m3 through a negatively charged pore (X -30 mol/m3, r_p
  0.40 nm, dx_e 2.5 um, eps_p 60) at J_v 5e-5 m/s and 298 K, Davies
  activities, no concentration polarisation.  The paper calls it "very
  stiff": the hindered diffusivities of Na+ (0.302e-9 m2/s) and Mg2+
  (0.429e-11) differ by two orders of magnitude, the Na+ permeate is 0.5 %
  of its feed, and a shooting method from a guessed permeate fails (its
  Fig. 5).  The ions are the paper's: Mg2+ 0.35 nm and 0.707e-9 m2/s
  (section 4.2), Na+ 0.184 nm / 1.33e-9 and Cl- 0.121 nm / 2.03e-9 from the
  ion database NanoFiltran ships, whose rows cite Bowen & Mohammad, AIChE
  J. 44 (1998) 1799 -- the same values the paper states in its section 4.1.
  The membrane IS NanoFiltran's shipped example (its INPUT.XLS).

  THE SIX OPERATIONS are Table 2's grid study at Choupo's own node counts:
  N10 ... N350, the pore on N nodes (N - 1 intervals; NanoFiltran's N
  counts the permeate node, so its N has N - 2 intervals inside the
  membrane).  N350 also writes the pore's profile (pore_N350.csv: c_i(x)
  and psi(x), the paper's Fig. 4).

  WHAT TABLE 2 TURNED OUT TO BE (measured; bin/curate/nanofiltran/
  regenerate_paper.py, docs/design/a-stiff-pore-solved-at-once.md section
  13).  NanoFiltran AS RECEIVED, on its shipped inputs with the paper's
  numerics (URF 0.2, TOL 1e-4), reproduces Table 2: the iteration counts
  (71 ... 88) exactly, Mg2+, Cl- and the potential to 2e-6, the trace Na+
  to 2.6e-5.  Its Davies evaluates ln gamma with the LOG10 constant -- and
  so do the paper's own Eqs. 12-13 as printed (Eq. 13's A carries the ln 10
  of a log10 constant, Eq. 12 is ln gamma) -- so ln gamma is ln 10 = 2.303
  times too small, and TABLE 2 CARRIES THAT FACTOR.  Choupo's
  `activity bulkOverPoreDavies` is Davies on log10; there is deliberately
  no option that reproduces the factor (a declared choice that reproduces
  a known error is a wrong model with a name).  So Choupo is NOT pinned to
  Table 2.  It is pinned (the `anchor` rows) to NanoFiltran built with the
  Davies constant corrected and the Born constant written in CODATA values
  (DAVIES_PATCH + BORN_PATCH), its diffusivity input matched (its Vogel
  correction of D to 298 K and its single-precision K_d literals cancelled
  through IONS.XLS), converged (TOL 1e-9) and Richardson-extrapolated from
  ND 102 and 204: agreement 7e-8 or better on all four numbers, Choupo
  extrapolated likewise.

  CHOUPO AGAINST TABLE 2 AT N = 350, the difference ACCOUNTED FOR one
  change at a time (NanoFiltran runs, regenerate_paper.py / paper.tsv):
                                             Na+        Mg2+      Cl-        dPsi_m [V]
    Table 2 (the paper)                      0.0483937  8.598782  17.245959  0.149754
    NanoFiltran as received                  0.0483949  8.598769  17.245933  0.149753
     + its Davies corrected (ln 10)          0.0535923  8.631082  17.315756  0.145844
     + its D input matched (Vogel, K_d)      0.0552122  8.630938  17.317088  0.145025
     + its Born constant in CODATA values    0.0554182  8.631751  17.318920  0.144930
     + converged (TOL 1e-4 -> 1e-9)          0.0554180  8.631751  17.318919  0.144930
     + grid extrapolated (ND 102/204)        0.0554248  8.631733  17.318890  0.144930
                                                                             (0.144960 in CODATA volts)
    Choupo, extrapolated (801/1601 nodes)    0.0554248  8.631732  17.318889  0.144960
    Choupo, op N350                          0.0554298  8.631692  17.318815  0.144958
  So Choupo puts 14.5 % more Na+ in the permeate than Table 2, 0.38 % more
  Mg2+ and 0.42 % more Cl-, at a potential 3.2 % lower.  The paper's Davies
  is +10.7 % of the Na+ and the whole of the potential's shift; the next
  +3.0 % is the DIFFUSIVITY: NanoFiltran corrects every D to T through its
  Vogel viscosity, and at the paper's 298 K (0.15 K below 25 C) that is
  0.38 % below the 25 C value.  Choupo's DSPM-DE corrects D0 to T by the
  same Stokes-Einstein ratio on the package's own liquid viscosity since
  C148 -- and this case declares NO liquid-viscosity model, so it reads the
  25 C D0 here, announced, and its anchors stay matched to it (with a Vogel
  model declared Choupo meets NanoFiltran's own D(T) to 1.1e-3 on the trace
  Na+, the residual NanoFiltran's literal 0.897e-3 Pa s: the design record,
  section 13.1); the Born constant's fifth figure is +0.4 %.  The trace
  Na+ amplifies every one of them: it is held by the Donnan field, and
  its permeate is a small difference of large migration and diffusion
  fluxes.

  THE POTENTIAL.  dPsi_m_V is the paper's Delta Psi_m: the permeate's
  potential against the feed-side solution at the wall (psi_m = 0, its Eq.
  24's reference) -- the two Donnan jumps included -- which is the
  permeate-node potential NanoFiltran writes.  Choupo's volts use CODATA R
  and F, NanoFiltran's 8.314 and 96500, so one dimensionless potential is
  2.08e-4 larger in Choupo's volts; the anchor is NanoFiltran's converted.

  THE GRID STUDY (permeate Na+ [mol/m3], Newton iterations):
               Table 2 (NanoFiltran, as published)   Choupo (correct Davies)
       N 10    0.0367468   71                         0.0627659    7
       N 20    0.0460774   75                         0.0571069    9
       N 50    0.0480736   79                         0.0556794    9
       N 100   0.0483219   82                         0.0554872   10
       N 200   0.0483809   85                         0.0554403   11
       N 350   0.0483937   88                         0.0554298   12
  Different discretisations converge from opposite sides: NanoFiltran's
  central differences from below, Choupo's exponentially fitted fluxes from
  above; both are second order.  Choupo's iterations are a full Newton with
  an analytic Jacobian from a local-Donnan seed; NanoFiltran's an
  under-relaxed (0.2) Newton to TOL 1e-4.

  WHAT KIND OF CHECK THIS IS.  Code-to-code verification (the anchors) and
  an ACCOUNTING of a published table: nothing here is a measurement.
